@* Tour is deliberately hardcoded English and NOT localized (spec 2026-08-12): its audience
   is external — other burn orgs evaluating the platform — not members. Do not wire it
   through a localizer or add resx keys in an i18n sweep. *@
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "What is Humans";
}

<section class="tour-hero">
    <div class="tour-hero-slideshow">
        <div class="tour-hero-slide active" style="background-image: url('/_content/Humans.Tour/img/hero-10.webp')"></div>
        <div class="tour-hero-slide" data-bg="/_content/Humans.Tour/img/hero-3.webp"></div>
        <div class="tour-hero-slide" data-bg="/_content/Humans.Tour/img/hero-13.webp"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="tour-hero-vignette" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="tour-hero-grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="tour-hero-ghost" aria-hidden="true">HUMANS</div>
    <canvas class="tour-hero-dust" id="tour-dust-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <div class="tour-hero-content">
        <p class="tour-hero-tag">Free &amp; open source &middot; built by burners, for burns</p>
        <span class="tour-hero-annotation" aria-hidden="true">goodbye, drawer full of spreadsheets</span>
        <h1>What is Humans? Your whole burn, in one place.</h1>
        <p class="tour-hero-lead">
            Humans is the membership platform that runs Nobodies Collective, the Spanish nonprofit
            behind the Elsewhere burn. It manages the full lifecycle of a burn organization —
            from a volunteer's first signup through teams, shifts, tickets, governance, and money —
            with GDPR built in and an audit trail the Board can actually read.
        </p>
        <div class="tour-hero-ctas">
            <a class="tour-btn tour-btn--solid" href="#people">See what's inside &darr;</a>
            <a class="tour-btn tour-btn--ghost" href="/About">The engineering story</a>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-hero-stats">
        <div class="tour-hero-stats-inner">
            <div class="tour-stat"><strong>2000+</strong><span>humans</span></div>
            <div class="tour-stat"><strong>40+</strong><span>sections</span></div>
            <div class="tour-stat"><strong>6</strong><span>languages</span></div>
            <div class="tour-stat"><strong>0</strong><span>spreadsheets</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-section" id="people">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">01 &mdash; the front door</span>
            <h2>People</h2>
            <p class="tour-section-lead">
                Everything starts with a person signing themselves up. From there, the system
                runs the paperwork so nobody else has to.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    Self-service signup, member profiles, and consent tracking. GDPR is built in,
                    not bolted on: every member can export their data or ask to be forgotten, and
                    the system handles both.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Onboarding runs itself — a new volunteer completes a profile, signs the
                    consents, and is provisioned automatically. No spreadsheet, no waiting on a
                    volunteer with admin rights to notice.
                </p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Self-service signup</strong><span>Profiles, consents, and automatic admission to the volunteer pool.</span></li>
                <li><strong>GDPR as a feature</strong><span>One-click data export and a real right-to-deletion path.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Automatic provisioning</strong><span>Accounts, teams, and Google Workspace access appear on their own.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-section tour-section--alt">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">02 &mdash; who does what, where, and when</span>
            <h2>Organize</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap tour-cap--flip anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    Teams with their own pages and Google Workspace resources, volunteer shift
                    scheduling with live coverage numbers, theme camps, city planning on a real
                    map, and a what's-on event guide.
                </p>
                <p>Visible to everyone — not locked in a coordinator's head.</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Teams</strong><span>Own pages, membership, and Workspace groups per team.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Shifts</strong><span>Scheduling with live coverage numbers, not guesswork.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Camps &amp; city planning</strong><span>Theme camps placed on a real map of the site.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Event guide</strong><span>A what's-on guide for everything happening at the burn.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-break" style="background-image: url('/_content/Humans.Tour/img/whatis-3.jpg')">
    <blockquote class="anim anim-fade-up">
        Built by the people who build the city.
        <span class="tour-annotation" aria-hidden="true">literally us, on our days off</span>
    </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="tour-section">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">03 &mdash; follow the money</span>
            <h2>Money</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    A members' store, expense reports with receipts and approval flow, per-team
                    budgets, and a live mirror of the association's accounting ledger.
                </p>
                <p>The Board sees where the money is without asking anyone to update a sheet.</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Members' store</strong><span>Merch and tickets sold inside the platform.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Expenses</strong><span>Receipts attached, approval flow, reimbursements tracked.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Budgets &amp; ledger</strong><span>Per-team budgets against a live accounting mirror.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-section tour-section--alt">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">04 &mdash; who approved that, and when</span>
            <h2>Govern</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap tour-cap--flip anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    Board roles with start and end dates, membership-tier applications with board
                    voting, and a full audit trail of every automated action.
                </p>
                <p>When someone asks "who approved that, and when" — the answer is one search away.</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Board roles</strong><span>Governance roles with real terms, not tribal memory.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Applications &amp; votes</strong><span>Membership tiers applied for, voted on, recorded.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Audit trail</strong><span>Every automated action leaves a record the Board can read.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-section">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">05 &mdash; six languages, one outbox</span>
            <h2>Communicate</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    Transactional email with a visible outbox, campaign sends to synced audiences,
                    and in-app notifications.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The whole member-facing UI ships in six languages — English, Spanish, Catalan,
                    German, French, and Italian — because a Spanish burn is not an English-only crowd.
                </p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Email outbox</strong><span>Every mail the system sends is visible and searchable.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Campaigns</strong><span>Bulk sends to audiences that sync themselves.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Six languages</strong><span>en &middot; es &middot; ca &middot; de &middot; fr &middot; it, enforced by tests.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-section tour-section--alt">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">06 &mdash; live data, not last Tuesday's printout</span>
            <h2>At the gate</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-cap tour-cap--flip anim anim-fade-up">
            <div class="tour-cap-copy">
                <p>
                    Ticketing with vendor imports and transfers, a gate check-in kiosk, barcode
                    scanning from a phone browser, and early-entry lists.
                </p>
                <p>The gate crew works from live data, in the desert, on whatever phone is charged.</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="tour-cap-list">
                <li><strong>Ticketing</strong><span>Vendor imports, transfers, and one source of truth.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Gate kiosk &amp; scanning</strong><span>Check-in with barcode scanning from a phone browser.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Early entry</strong><span>Build-week lists that match who is actually allowed in.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-break" style="background-image: url('/_content/Humans.Tour/img/whatis-2.jpg')">
    <blockquote class="anim anim-fade-up">
        Over 2,000 real people trust this system — and a volunteer Board relies on it to run
        a legal Spanish asociaci&oacute;n.
    </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="tour-section tour-section--dark">
    <div class="tour-container">
        <div class="anim anim-fade-up">
            <span class="tour-kicker">Humans for your burn</span>
            <h2>Take the parts your burn needs.</h2>
            <p class="tour-section-lead">
                Humans is built as independent sections — People, Teams, Shifts, Tickets, Store,
                Expenses, Governance, and more — each owning its own data and talking to the
                others through narrow interfaces. That structure is the roadmap: a deployment per
                burn, configured with the sections your org actually uses. Run People + Shifts +
                Gate and skip the rest. Use Microsoft 365 instead of Google Workspace. Add your
                language. The modularity is real today; per-burn configuration is where the
                project is headed.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tour-eng-grid anim anim-fade-up">
            <div><strong>Not vibe-coded</strong><span>Strict layering and section isolation, enforced by custom Roslyn analyzers at the line of code.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Tested like it matters</strong><span>Per-section test suites, architecture tests, and mutation testing on the rules that count.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Nothing invisible</strong><span>Automation that acts without leaving an audit entry is treated as a bug.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Actually open</strong><span>AGPL-3.0, developed in the open, section by section.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="anim anim-fade-up" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            Curious how it's built — and how well? <a href="/About">Read the engineering story</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="tour-close">
    <div class="tour-container anim anim-fade-up">
        <span class="tour-hero-annotation" aria-hidden="true">yes, it runs a real burn</span>
        <h2>Bring Humans to your burn.</h2>
        <p class="tour-section-lead" style="margin: 0 auto;">
            We built this for ourselves and we would love for it to run your burn too.
            Write to us — we are volunteers, we get it.
        </p>
        <div class="tour-close-ctas">
            <a class="tour-btn tour-btn--solid" href="mailto:humans@nobodies.team">humans@nobodies.team</a>
            <a class="tour-btn tour-btn--outline" href="https://github.com/nobodies-collective/Humans" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<footer class="tour-footer">
    <div class="tour-container">
        Humans &middot; free and open source (AGPL-3.0) &middot; built by burners, for burns
    </div>
</footer>
